  <div id="tab-how-it-works" class="tab-panel">
    <div class="info-card">
      <h2 style="font-size: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">1. The Core Architecture of TOTP (RFC 6238)</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        Time-Based One-Time Password (TOTP) is an open internet standard (RFC 6238) extension of HMAC-Based One-Time Password (HOTP, RFC 4226). Instead of using an incrementing sequence counter, it uses the <strong>current Unix timestamp</strong> divided into 30-second intervals as the counter.
      </p>

      <div style="background: #f1f5f9; border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); padding: 1rem 1.25rem; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        <h4 style="font-size: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">Mathematical Formula</h4>
        <code style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 600; color: #1e40af;">
          Counter (C) = ⌊ (Current Unix Time - T0) / X ⌋
        </code>
        <p style="font-size: 0.8125rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-top: 0.35rem;">
          Where <code>T0 = 0</code> (Unix Epoch Jan 1, 1970) and time-step <code>X = 30 seconds</code>.
          Then: <code>OTP = Truncate(HMAC-SHA-1(Shared_Secret, Counter)) mod 10^6</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="font-size: 1.25rem; margin-top: 2rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">The Complete Lifecycle</h3>

      <div class="flow-step-box">
        <div class="flow-step-num">1</div>
        <div>
          <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">Secret Generation &amp; QR Exchange</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.875rem;">
            When a user requests 2FA setup, the server generates a cryptographically random 20-byte key (Base32 encoded). The server bundles this secret into a standardized URI:
            <code>otpauth://totp/AppName:user@email.com?secret=BASE32KEY&amp;issuer=AppName</code>.
            This URI is rendered as a QR code for quick scanning with mobile authenticators (Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator, 1Password, Authy).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flow-step-box">
        <div class="flow-step-num">2</div>
        <div>
          <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">Synchronized Autonomous Calculation</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.875rem;">
            No internet connection or SMS network is needed! The user's authenticator app and the server both know the <strong>same shared secret</strong> and the <strong>current time</strong>. Every 30 seconds, both independently hash the timestamp with the secret, producing the exact same 6-digit code.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flow-step-box">
        <div class="flow-step-num">3</div>
        <div>
          <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">Clock Drift Window Tolerance</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.875rem;">
            Because the client phone clock and server clock might differ by a few seconds, the server uses a verification window of <code>window: 1</code>. This verifies tokens from <code>[now - 30s, now, now + 30s]</code> (a 90-second total validity window), avoiding frustrating false rejections.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flow-step-box">
        <div class="flow-step-num">4</div>
        <div>
          <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">Authenticated Encryption at Rest (AES-256-GCM)</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.875rem;">
            The shared secret key must <strong>never</strong> be stored in plaintext in the database. If an attacker dumps the database, plaintext secrets would compromise all accounts. This application encrypts the secret using AES-256-GCM with a unique 96-bit initialization vector (IV) and a 128-bit authentication tag before saving to MongoDB.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flow-step-box">
        <div class="flow-step-num">5</div>
        <div>
          <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 0.25rem;">Single-Use Emergency Recovery Codes</h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.875rem;">
            If users lose or damage their phone, they could be permanently locked out. The app generates 10 high-entropy recovery codes (e.g. <code>ABCD-1234</code>). Codes are hashed using <strong>bcrypt</strong> (just like passwords) and marked consumed upon use.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

